<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/843">843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Regnum Teutonicum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Regnum Teutonicum) was the original core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, distinct from the empire's kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. It grew out of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> formed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 and was made up of the stem duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Swabia, consolidating its hold on Lotharingia in the 10th century. After the last Carolingian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, died in 911, the dukes elected Conrad I king, and from 962 the kings of Germany claimed the imperial title. Its rulers stressed their imperial office, and no official royal title named Germany until 1508.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Near the Linones stronghold of Lenzen, a Saxon army of the <b>Kingdom of Germany</b> under the magnate Bernhard destroyed an army of the Slavic Redarii.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
